# Git: push after commit

The user backs up this project on GitHub (`origin`) and will continue it on
another machine, so commits must reach the remote, not just local history.

- After creating any commit the user asked for, **also push** it to `origin`
  on the current branch (e.g. `git push` once upstream is set, otherwise
  `git push -u origin <branch>`).
- Treat a commit as incomplete until it is pushed; report the push result.
- Still follow the git safety protocol: never force-push, never push to
  `main`/`master` without an explicit request, and only commit when asked.
- Pushing a normal feature branch is fine without extra confirmation; pause and
  ask before anything destructive or history-rewriting.
